@page
@model CommunityHub.Pages.Organizer.TelemetryModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Attendee telemetry";
}

@* §55: in-area organizer telemetry. Reuses the SAME shared panel partial as the
   public /attendee-telemetry page and the sponsor page, so the ranked tables +
   segment + topic-filter dropdowns are identical everywhere. *@
<div class="card tl-wrap">
    <h1 id="telemetry">Who's coming to Experts Live Denmark</h1>

    @if (Model.AccessDenied)
    {
        <p class="error">This area is for organizers only.</p>
        <p><a asp-page="/Index">@Localizer["Common.BackToHub"]</a></p>
        return;
    }

    @* 🔴 §1052 — THIS SENTENCE SAID "Same figures as the public sponsor telemetry page" WHILE THIS
       PAGE SHOWED A TABLE THE PUBLIC PAGE DOES NOT HAVE ("Top companies", §69).
       That is how a correctly-gated organizer table got reported as a public GDPR leak: all three
       telemetry surfaces share this panel and the same H1, so the ONLY thing distinguishing them
       was a claim of parity that was false. Reading company names under that sentence is a correct
       inference from a wrong premise — the defect was the caption, not the data.
       🔒 So it now names the difference explicitly, and says who can see it. A page that tells you
       what is public must be right about it, or it is worse than silent. *@
    <p style="color:#4b5563;margin-top:0;">
        Aggregate attendee analytics — pick a segment and drill into the breakdown by
        job role, where attendees are from, ticket type and the registration custom-field
        topics. The public and sponsor telemetry pages show these same aggregates
        <strong>except “Top companies”</strong>, which is organizer-only and is never built for
        those pages.
    </p>

    <partial name="_AttendeeTelemetryPanel"
             model="new CommunityHub.Pages.Shared.AttendeeTelemetryPanelModel(Model.Data, Model.Segments, IsOrganizer: true)" />
</div>
